{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":299.9800109863281,"text":"you already have the President of the United States having a completely opposite end-of-spectrum reaction to it. Then the other challenge is, just even if the US could decide that the labs here are going to slow down, doesn't mean that the labs outside of the US do as well, and then that potentially becomes a threat to the technology and how the technology is used, and are the people in the US okay with it? It's overall a very complicated issue. I remember even hearing an interview with Dario last year where he talked about how obviously his views maybe changed, but he said that he didn't feel like they could afford to slow down or pace things at that time because they had a head start on say China and other areas of the world, but it wasn't that big a head start. If they were multiple years ahead, then they could perhaps slow down to make sure things were safer overall. But because there's this external threat, then they can't slow down the pace essentially. I guess things have changed somewhat, maybe they've left the head far enough, or maybe the threat is big enough or real enough. But all of this has also been self-reported when you look at the stuff that the labs have come out with in terms of where there's been a breach or someone has declared some issue. If that happens in things like airlines, like there's some incident, there's third parties today that go and investigate what actually happened. We just don't have any of those checks and balances. It feels like something is necessary. I guess overall, I would say it's at least a start to something that probably needs to be done. I don't know enough about how some of these other things work in other industries to say is this the right approach or not? But it definitely feels like something needs to be done. Because if you look at almost any other area where public safety is a potential issue, whether that's making a vehicle or flying a plane, designing a drug, there is multiple checks and balances before you're able to do any of these things. I can't just go create a car and sell it on my own and people will come after me. There's things that prevent you from just doing that. Yeah, that's a good analogy. It's always like, where's the tipping point between something that, you know, until, in many areas, you can just create software and sell it and no one's like, oh, but what if that software does X or Y? Unless it is something that clearly crosses a legal boundary, it could still be doing something quite terrible as software or cause damage. But it's kind of encapsulated in like, well, if a company causes damage, that's like a liability and that's like a civil thing. Whereas, I mean, a different example, I guess, is when crypto all took off and basically financial regulation had to come in because they thought that could cause damage to the average person from a financial standpoint, like if you're being misled as to what you are doing. And yeah, so now in this case, it's like, should there be regulation on top of just developing a model because of all the ways a model can be used? Yeah, I think that's a, yeah, so the car, the airplane example is interesting, where you can't just, I mean, you can design it, you can build it, but you can't sell it to anyone. Yeah, I can't just start offering commercial flights. Exactly, yeah. So, well, I think that's a really good place to leave it, actually. Hopefully that's been kind of helpful, a bit more, I don't want to say theoretical, but yeah, obviously just like looking through why has any of this even come about and like where are things going in the last, I guess, few weeks, few months in terms of where the Frontier Labs especially seem to be kind of positioning themselves on this, so. Yeah, I do want to say, like, you know, I'm firmly on the side that we've already opened the, I don't know, the Pandora's box, like there's no sort of, you know, putting it back in at this point that the innovations are going to come. It's like this tidal wave is coming, and like you could either try to swim to shore and escape the tidal wave, get the high ground, or you can, you know, try to grab a surfboard and surf that tidal wave. And I'm much more on the surfboard side of like, let's figure this out. Like, there's, I think, a tremendous amount of potential upside, like the light side of the technology that there's too much upside to ignore. But let's figure out a way to also make sure that the dark side doesn't end up taking over the upside that we can look forward to. Yeah, absolutely. Well, talking of upsides, how can you use highlights? Yes, absolutely. Good time to move on to, yeah, usually our sort of favorite part of the show. Do you want to kick us off, Sean? What did you come across as one of your favorites in the last week? Yeah, so there was this one, I really like the title of it, it's called Pirating the Pirates, which was posted by Piotr Grabowski, I think.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":3.200000047683716,"text":" you already have the President of the United States","tokens":[50364,291,1217,362,264,3117,295,264,2824,3040,50524],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.31768888235092163,"compression_ratio":1.6691449880599976,"no_speech_prob":0.004398321732878685},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":3.200000047683716,"end":6.760000228881836,"text":" having a completely opposite end-of-spectrum reaction to it.","tokens":[50524,1419,257,2584,6182,917,12,2670,12,82,1043,6247,5480,281,309,13,50702],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.31768888235092163,"compression_ratio":1.6691449880599976,"no_speech_prob":0.004398321732878685},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":6.760000228881836,"end":9.199999809265137,"text":" Then the other challenge is,","tokens":[50702,1396,264,661,3430,307,11,50824],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.31768888235092163,"compression_ratio":1.6691449880599976,"no_speech_prob":0.004398321732878685},{"id":3,"se